Conclusion & Limitations  

5.1 Conclusion 

Throughout this study, we examined three main themes: computational cost, explanation quality, and model-dependent trade-offs, addressed through three research questions. 
Firstly, runtime increased with both perturbation sample size (N) and feature dimensionality (d), with Logistic Regression consistently the fastest, followed by Random Forest 
and XGBoost. Secondly, increasing N generally improved explanation fidelity and stability, although these improvements diminished at higher sample counts, while increasing 
dimensionality often reduced explanation consistency. Thirdly, the underlying model significantly influenced the cost-quality trade-off. Our experiments showed that XGBoost 
achieved the highest mean held-out fidelity (R²) and explanation stability at 300–500 features, while Random Forest produced lower absolute surrogate error (RMSE) at these 
dimensions. Logistic Regression offered the lowest computational cost but generally weaker explanation quality. Overall, XGBoost demonstrated the strongest quality-focused 
performance in our higher-dimensional experiments, although no single model consistently outperformed the others across all metrics. 

5.2 Limitations 

Several limitations affected the scope of this study. TabFM was excluded due to its computational demands, with predictions alone estimated to require approximately ~34 hours. 
The original Covertype dataset was replaced because its 54 features were insufficient for our intended high-dimensional analysis. Spearman correlation was removed because its 
model-dependent chance levels complicated stability comparisons, leaving top-five Jaccard as our repeatability measure. Furthermore, the study was restricted to one dataset, 
UCI HAR, and 30 explained instances. The dataset's highly correlated sensor features may also produce unrealistic LIME perturbations. These factors limit the generalisability 
of our findings to other datasets, models, and experimental configurations. 
